ocess of externalizing your  thoughts and it helps clarify your  entire creative process to get that  writing published drawing right visual  thinking explaining things making  diagrams making abstract ideas more  tangible performing okay now it starts  to get performing right but I think when  you take that step to get on a stage to  really stand in front of people and say  I know what I'm talking about your  knowledge what you know your performance  your productivity takes on a whole new  level because you're forced to stand for  something so it could include I mean  nothing that crazy speaking at an event  speaking at a meet-up or recording a  video don't even have to get in front of  people  we have YouTube and periscope and  Twitter and all these things producing  right actually publishing releasing  early and releasing often to the outside  work to the outside world summarizing or  condensing someone else's ideas  interpreting them or critiquing them or  Trant even just translating from one  medium to another and finally selling  right when you put yourself and your  work on the line to sell them and I mean  selling for money but also selling your  ideas teaching or selling your projects  which is collaborating right this kind  of a spectrum from from like more  private to more public less engaged to  more engaged right this is what I think  composability looks like in the broader  world of knowledge work and it's  something most knowledge workers do not  do right yeah we have blogs social media  is pushing everyone in this direction  but what would it look like if we were  all  constantly pushing out valuable work to  help others learn to help others advance  to clarify our own thinking the thinking  of others I think the world would look  very different and I think the benefits  this would bring to knowledge work are  some of the same benefits that you've  experienced over the last years in  software development abstraction right  imagine if kind of general 